J. Pedro Sousa do Amaral wrote:

>Another question is about bogometer benchmarking. How useful is to use
>the function time to benchmark the performance of a given program
>compiled across different machines? From the man time: "The time
>utility executes and times utility. After the utility finishes, time
>writes the total time elapsed, the time consumed by system overhead,
>and the time used to execute utility to the standard error stream."
>What is exactly the meaning of the two last times reported?

The CPU time used by system overhead is usually for I/O operations.
Most I/O in most flavors of UNIX is still buffered, so there is copying
between system memory and user memory before the data is actually
transferred to/from the I/O devices.  The user CPU time is the the
actual amount of time yoru program spent using up CPU cycles doing
whatever it does.  A lot of times, the sum of these two are less than
the elapsed time, and the difference is usually due to the delay in
I/O operations.  Moving data to/from physical devices usually takes
orders of magnitude longer than moving it between memory, or just
processing the data.  This is why you can't reliably predict the
performance of your program based just on CPU or memory speeds.

eyc




