I now have software raid working,
but not without some problems getting there:
I tried the pristine 2.2.16 kernel with
the Red Hat patches and the Red Hat 2.2.16
kernel and in both cases I ran into problems
when booting because I had already made an
/etc/raidtab file.  This file should probably not
be there prior to running mkraid.
I had to rename /etc/raidtab to something  else,
reboot, reestablish /etc/raidtab, and then mkraid /dev/md0...
fdisk /dev/md0....mkfs...mount....

I'm using the RedHat 2.2.16 aright now.



Robert Williams wrote:

> Mike Tibor wrote:
>
> > On Thu, 16 Nov 2000, Robert Williams wrote:
> >
> > > I've been trying to get software raid working
> > > on an UP1000.
> >
> > Are you using the stock Red Hat kernel, or one that you built from
> > pristine sources?  If not the stock RH kernel, one of the raid patches
> > from http://people.redhat.com/mingo/raid-patches/ should be applied.
>
> pristine sources...   So these patches at redhat will apply
> to 2.14 or 2.16?    I've tried patches available for software raid
> from other sites, but they only go up to 2.11, and they didn't work
> on 2.14.
>
> > If you've already done the above, you might try doing a "raidstart
> > /dev/md0".  You shouldn't have to do that since you're using persistent
> > superblocks, but oddly enough that was the only thing that worked for me
> > in a similar situation using pristine 2.2.16+raid-2.2.16-A0 on a RH 6.1
> > box.
>
> Thanks.   I'll give it a shot.
>
